import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Retro — premise-anchored derivable: the composite's minting proof counts","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro — premise-anchored \`derivable\`: the composite's minting proof counts","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/premise-anchored-derivability.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/premise-anchored-derivability.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/premise-anchored-derivability.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-premise-anchored-derivable-the-composite-s-minting-proof-counts" tabindex="-1">Retro — premise-anchored <code>derivable</code>: the composite&#39;s minting proof counts <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-premise-anchored-derivable-the-composite-s-minting-proof-counts" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — premise-anchored derivable: the composite&#39;s minting proof counts”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Follows</strong>: <code>binding-env-cap.md</code> (its named policy question: &quot;should <code>corpus.base_rules</code> carry a stored object&#39;s premise closure, so <code>derivable</code> reflects the premise-anchored proof?&quot;) and <code>lemma-cert-admission.md</code>. <strong>Commit</strong>: this commit&#39;s parent — <code>evidence: premise-anchored derivability for stored composites</code>.</p><h2 id="the-decision" tabindex="-1">The decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-decision" aria-label="Permalink to “The decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Yes — with a site-verification guard.</strong> A stored composite&#39;s <code>derivable</code> leg now evaluates against <code>corpus.base_rules</code> plus its <strong>transitive premise closure</strong>: the record&#39;s <code>guard.parents</code> field resolved through the store member-wise — the same resolution <code>admit_object</code> performs for guard transport (<code>ca42c16</code>): each premise must be a row of the same store that rebuilds at <em>full declared strength</em> (<code>missing_hooks</code> minus <code>transported</code> empty), or — when it names a base rule — the axiom itself. A member that resolves short of that bar (no row, weaker-than-declared admit, cycle) empties the whole closure: a partial premise set is not the derivation the object was minted under.</p><p>The alternative — axioms-only <code>derivable</code> — is <em>sound but blind</em>: it under-reports a composite whose entire honest claim is &quot;derivable under the premises I was minted from&quot;. The alternative the question warns against — unconditionally trusting the closure — is circular: <code>verify_law</code>&#39;s certificate proves each step is a valid <em>application</em> of a named rule, not that the named rule is <em>true</em>. A false stored rule in the closure would let a false composite fake derivability.</p><h2 id="the-anti-gaming-bar" tabindex="-1">The anti-gaming bar <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-anti-gaming-bar" aria-label="Permalink to “The anti-gaming bar”">​</a></h2><p>The anchored credit is granted only when the reported certificate is standalone-replayable <strong>and every step naming a non-axiom rule site-verifies</strong>: <code>eval_instance(step.lhs, step.rhs)</code> on the recorded concrete instance — the exact terms the premise rewrote between — under the same fp64 random-env oracle <code>num_true</code> uses. A premise true at the site the proof uses is sufficient (<code>derivable</code> is a per-instance verdict); a premise false at its applied site, an unverifiable step, or a non-replayable witness all revoke the credit — <code>measure</code> tries <code>base_rules</code> first, so the revoked verdict falls back to the honest axiom-only answer. The flag surfaces as <code>Evidence.premise_anchored</code> and <code>derivable=True(premise-anchored)</code> in the truth-gate detail.</p><h2 id="re-measured-the-conv-bn-composite" tabindex="-1">Re-measured: the conv+BN composite <a class="header-anchor" href="#re-measured-the-conv-bn-composite" aria-label="Permalink to “Re-measured: the conv+BN composite”">​</a></h2><p><code>lab/probes/conv_fold_premise_pay_probe.py</code>, same mint (974 emitted, 8-rule premise closure, one-case corpus). Measured at <code>819beeb</code> — which also landed the joint-shape schemata that unstarve the sweep — the composite&#39;s account is:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>stage</th><th>before (<code>0721cec</code>)</th><th>now</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>measure</td><td><code>derivable=False</code> under <code>ALL_RULES</code></td><td><code>derivable=True(premise-anchored)</code> — replayable cert, premise steps <code>conv_channel_shift_sp</code> + <code>syn_…_209</code> site-verify</td></tr><tr><td>truth</td><td><strong>FAIL</strong> (starved sweep, no waiver)</td><td>pass on <em>both</em> legs — <code>numeric=True</code>, real site <code>1eq/0ne</code>, and the joint-shape banks now reach the corner: synth <code>105eq/0ne</code> in a 360-env window (was <code>0 accepted / 2000 declined</code>); the anchored derivation is present as the waiver it would still need were the sweep starved</td></tr><tr><td>typed-pay</td><td>not reached</td><td>pass — <code>fires=1 paid=1 verify_fail=0</code></td></tr><tr><td>closure</td><td>not reached</td><td><strong>FAIL</strong> — <code>enode=6.00x</code> vs the 2.0 cap</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The gauntlet now refuses at <strong>closure</strong>, not truth: the fold expands the real conv+BN term into its spelled form inside the probe e-graph (6× enode growth on a one-term graph). That is a <em>different</em>, honest gate — expansion cost — and the correct residual blocker: the object is proved (premise-anchored), measured (105 guarded equals), and pays (17 438.7 vs 34 874.3), but its closure safety is a corpus-level property the anchor does not touch. Whether an expansion composite should carry a different closure bar is the next named policy question — deliberately not decided here.</p><h2 id="what-the-tests-pin" tabindex="-1">What the tests pin <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-tests-pin" aria-label="Permalink to “What the tests pin”">​</a></h2><p><code>tests/test_discovery_gauntlet.py</code> (+6): the flip on a minted <code>square→pow</code> composite under an axiom-thin corpus; transitivity through a stored <code>syn_*</code> intermediate; refusal when a premise row is absent or admits weaker than declared; axiom-parents resolving from <code>base_rules</code> without a store row; a <strong>false-premise pair</strong> that merges the e-graph yet fails site verification; and the audit&#39;s unit edges (no cert, non-replayable, axioms-only).</p><h2 id="what-stays-open" tabindex="-1">What stays open <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays-open" aria-label="Permalink to “What stays open”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The conv+BN composite&#39;s <code>closure</code> refusal (6.00× &gt; 2.0 cap) — expansion-composite policy, not derivability.</li><li><code>premise_anchored</code> does not reach the verdicts table: the gauntlet does not record verdict rows, so nothing persists a stale-anchored <code>derivable</code>; a future cached-verdict path for stored objects must carry the anchor scope in its key.</li></ul><p>Gate account: <code>tests/test_discovery_gauntlet.py</code>, <code>test_discovery_evidence.py</code>, <code>test_discovery_pipeline.py</code> green; ruff/format/ty clean on touched files.</p>`,16)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};